= Contradiction =

== Overview ==
A '''contradiction''' is a logical conflict in which statements cannot jointly hold under the relevant logic.

In classical propositional logic, a standard form is:

<pre>
φ ∧ ¬φ
</pre>

a statement together with its negation.

== Contradiction and disagreement ==
Two people can disagree without their claims being logically contradictory.

Claims are contradictory when the truth of one excludes the truth of the other in the relevant formalization.

Ordinary language can hide ambiguity, scope, time, or context differences that make apparent contradictions disappear.

== Contradiction and inconsistency ==
A theory containing or deriving a contradiction is classically inconsistent.

In classical logic, inconsistency supports '''explosion''': arbitrary conclusions can be derived.

Paraconsistent logics are designed to block unrestricted explosion, so contradiction and total triviality are not equivalent in every logic.

== Proof by contradiction ==
A proof can assume the negation of a target claim and derive a contradiction.

Under suitable classical rules, that contradiction supports the original claim.

The acceptability and exact form of proof by contradiction can vary across logical systems.

== Contradictory and contrary claims ==
Contradictory claims cannot both be true and cannot both be false.

Contrary claims cannot both be true but may both be false.

This distinction matters in traditional logic and ordinary-language analysis.

